window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"10-12","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"10-12-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-01/","title_he":"א – איסור ברכה לבטלה","title_en":"The Prohibition of a Berakha Le-vatala (Blessing in Vain)","body_en":"<p>A person is permitted to pray and to praise God in any language he wishes and at any time he wishes, and within prayers and praises it is permitted to mention His holy names, but it is forbidden to recite a berakha le-vatala (a blessing in vain). What is a berakha le-vatala? When one recites a blessing where it does not belong. For example, one who recites Ha-motzi without eating bread, or one who recites Birkat Ha-mazon without having eaten bread beforehand. Likewise, if one recited Ha-motzi and began eating bread, and then recited Ha-motzi again, since he already fulfilled his obligation with the first blessing, the second blessing is in vain. Likewise, one who ate bread and recited Birkat Ha-mazon, if he recites Birkat Ha-mazon again, his blessing will be in vain.</p><p>And although in practice, when he recited a blessing in vain he said words of praise and thanksgiving to God, since the Sages instituted that the blessing be recited under specific conditions and he recited it where it does not belong, he has transgressed a rabbinic prohibition. The matter is similar to one who swears a 'shevu'at shav' (a vain oath), for example, one who swears that the heavens are the heavens: although he did not utter a falsehood, since he used the language of an oath for a well-known matter about which there is no need to swear, he has transgressed a Torah prohibition, as it is stated (Shemot 20:6): \"You shall not take the name of the Lord your God in vain.\" Likewise, one who recites a blessing at a time when he does not need to recite it has nullified the purpose of the blessing, for the Sages instituted the recitation of the sanctified text of the blessing in the place where a person needs to thank his Creator and bless Him, and he has disregarded their enactment and recited the blessing in vain. However, since the enactment of the blessings is rabbinic, one who recites them where they do not belong transgresses a rabbinic prohibition. And there is an opinion that one who recites a berakha le-vatala transgresses a Torah prohibition.<sup>1</sup></p><p>If one erred and recited a blessing in vain, he should immediately say: \"Barukh shem kevod malkhuto le-olam va-ed\" (\"Blessed be the name of His glorious kingdom forever and ever\"). There is a certain rectification in this, for by blessing the name of His glorious kingdom, he somewhat dispels the desecration of God's name that he caused when he recited a blessing in vain. And if he said \"Barukh Ata Hashem\" (\"Blessed are You, Lord\") and remembered that he does not need to recite a blessing, he should conclude \"lamdeni ḥukekha\" (\"teach me Your statutes\"), and thus it turns out that he recited a complete verse from Tehillim (119:12), and he has not committed the prohibition of a berakha le-vatala (SA 206:6).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"One who utters God's name without meaning transgresses a positive commandment, as it is stated (Devarim 6:13): \"You shall fear the Lord your God,\" and one who utters His name in vain does not fear Him (Temura 4a). But it is permitted to say praises and thanksgiving before Him and to mention His holy names in the course of this (see Peninei Halakha: Likutim vol. 1, 5:16). There is a further negative prohibition against swearing a vain oath, as it is stated (Shemot 20:6): \"You shall not take the name of the Lord your God in vain, for the Lord will not hold guiltless one who takes His name in vain.\" One of the four types of vain oath is an oath about a known matter, for example, one who swears about the heavens that they are the heavens. The Sages said (Berakhot 33a): \"Whoever recites an unnecessary blessing transgresses 'You shall not take [the name of the Lord your God in vain]'\" (Shemot 20:6). According to most poskim, among them Rabbeinu Tam, Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona, the Rosh, the Rid and the Ritva, the prohibition is rabbinic, and they based it on the Torah prohibition of a vain oath. According to the Rambam (Hilkhot Berakhot 1:15), the prohibition is from the Torah, for one who recites a blessing where it is not needed is considered as one who swears in vain. Some explain that even according to the Rambam the prohibition is rabbinic (Eliya Rabba 215:5), but the opinion of most Aḥaronim is that according to the Rambam the prohibition is from the Torah (MA 215:6; MB 20)."}]},{"slug":"10-12-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-02/","title_he":"ב – ברכה שאינה צריכה","title_en":"An Unnecessary Blessing","body_en":"<p>In addition to the prohibition of reciting a berakha le-vatala, there is also a prohibition of causing an unnecessary blessing (berakha she-eina tzerikha). For example, one who wishes to eat an apple and a pear, since the blessing of both is the same, must recite one blessing before eating them and one after eating them. If, after he finishes eating the apple, he recites Borei Nefashot so that he will thereby need to recite Ha-etz and Borei Nefashot again on the pear, it turns out that he has caused himself to add two blessings. It is forbidden to do this, because a blessing is a precious and sanctified thing; when it is said for a need it adds blessing, and when it is said without need, its value is impaired.</p><p>However, this prohibition is less severe, and therefore when a person has a doubt in matters of blessings, and by causing an additional blessing he will resolve the doubt, he is permitted to cause an additional blessing. For example, when it is clear that a certain food is secondary (tafel) to another, one recites the blessing on the primary food (ikar) and exempts the secondary one, and if he recites a blessing on the secondary food separately and on the primary food separately, he transgresses the prohibition, for he causes himself to recite an unnecessary blessing. But when there is a doubt whether the food is indeed secondary to the other, it is preferable that he first eat each of the two foods separately, so that he will recite its own blessing over each one. And although by eating each food separately he causes himself to recite an additional blessing, there is no prohibition of an unnecessary blessing in this, since he does so in order to resolve the doubt (above 11:2).</p><p>Likewise, it is permitted to cause additional blessings in order to complete one hundred blessings on Shabbat, provided that he does so in a manner that appears natural to the order of his eating. For example, it is permitted to ask that the fruit be brought after the meal, whereby he will gain another blessing. For if he eats them during the meal, he will recite Ha-etz over them at the beginning but will not recite a final blessing over them, since Birkat Ha-mazon will exempt them as well. But if he eats the fruit after the meal, he will recite a blessing over them at the beginning and at the end. There is no prohibition in this, since on a weekday too, people sometimes postpone eating fruit until after the meal. However, on a weekday it is permitted to do this only when one genuinely wishes to make a break between the meal and eating the fruit, whereas on Shabbat it is permitted to do this even when the main aim is to complete one hundred blessings. Likewise, it is permitted to eat mezonot and fruit before the Shabbat meal in order to complete one hundred blessings, provided that he pauses for half an hour, or at least a quarter of an hour, between eating them and the beginning of the meal, for then he does not appear as one who is interested only in adding blessings, but as one who wishes to delight in Shabbat with many tastings (see above 3:12).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"When causing the blessing is done out of the order of eating in order to add a blessing, according to all opinions it is forbidden. Therefore, even for the sake of completing one hundred blessings, it is forbidden to stop in the middle of eating, recite a final blessing, and then recite a first blessing again in order to continue eating. Likewise, it is forbidden for a person who wishes to eat an apple and a pear to intend in his blessing over the apple to cover the apple alone, so that afterwards he will need to recite a blessing again over the pear. And even on Shabbat, when he needs to add blessings in order to complete one hundred blessings, it is forbidden to do this, for it is plainly evident that his aim is only to recite another blessing. But for the sake of se'uda shelishit (the third Shabbat meal) it is permitted: if he is in a situation where, if he does not divide his meal in two, he will not manage to eat se'uda shelishit afterwards, he is permitted to stop in the middle of his meal and recite Birkat Ha-mazon. Tosafot are indeed stringent about this, but the Rosh is lenient for the sake of se'uda shelishit, and so it is ruled in SA 291:3; see there. There is no prohibition of causing an unnecessary blessing in this, since he does not do so in order to add blessings but in order to hold another meal, and consequently he must recite a blessing over it. When causing the additional blessing is done indirectly, such as by eating the fruit before the meal or postponing it until after the meal, or dividing their eating into different times, since a person sometimes does this anyway of his own wish, in which case it is permitted according to all opinions, it is permitted to do this on Shabbat in order to complete one hundred blessings. According to the MA 215:6 it is forbidden, since his main intention is to add blessings. But according to the Shela, the Maharam Galanti and others, since it is possible that he would do this also because it is more pleasant for him, it is permitted to do this on Shabbat in order to complete one hundred blessings. And if he takes care to make a break between the meal and eating the fruit and sweets after it, it is permitted according to all opinions. And before the meal, in any case, one must make a break between the tastings and the meal, for otherwise there is a doubt regarding the final blessing, as explained above 3:12. When the aim is to resolve a doubt, as for example in a doubt regarding primary and secondary foods, it is permitted to separate the kinds and thereby cause an unnecessary blessing, because it is more serious to enter a doubt of eating without a blessing than to enter a doubt of causing an unnecessary blessing (and so it is evident in SA 174:7; see above 3, note 7; 11:2, and in note 2 regarding the opinion of the Gra\"z)."}]},{"slug":"10-12-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-03/","title_he":"ג – ספק ברכות להקל","title_en":"In Doubtful Cases of Blessings We Are Lenient","body_en":"<p>When there is a doubt whether one must recite a certain blessing, there is no obligation to recite it, for we hold the rule that whenever we have a doubt concerning a rabbinic matter, the halakha is lenient (safeka de-rabbanan le-kula). And since the enactment of the blessings is rabbinic, there is no obligation to recite a blessing where there is a doubt. And if one wishes to volunteer and recite the blessing, he enters a doubtful prohibition, for we have learned that it is forbidden to recite a blessing in vain. This is the meaning of the rule \"safek berakhot le-hakel\" (\"in doubtful cases of blessings we are lenient\"): when there is a doubt whether one must recite a certain blessing, one should be lenient, and it is forbidden to be stringent and recite the blessing. For example, a person who is in doubt whether he ate a kezayit, the amount that obligates a final blessing, should not recite the blessing.<sup>3</sup></p><p>Some say that only when the doubt is more or less evenly balanced, for example when close to half of the poskim hold that one should not recite the blessing, is it forbidden to recite it. But if, according to a clear majority of the poskim, one must recite the blessing, one recites it and does not take into account the minority opinion. In contrast, others say that even when only a minority of the poskim hold that one should not recite the blessing, there is still a doubt here, and it is forbidden to recite it. And this is the accepted ruling. That is, the matter of a blessing must be absolute, so that a person stands with certainty before his Creator and blesses; but when he is in doubt, he cannot say a blessing before his Creator.</p><p>All this concern for the opinion of poskim who hold that one should not recite a blessing applies only when there is no clear custom; but if there is a custom to recite the blessing, one recites it in accordance with the custom, for the very fact that people were accustomed to recite it shows that this is how the halakha was decided, and there is no longer a doubt in it.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"If one ate bread and was satiated, the obligation of Birkat Ha-mazon is from the Torah, and consequently, if a doubt arose, he must recite it, for \"safeka de-oraita le-ḥumra\" (\"a doubt concerning a Torah law is resolved stringently\"), as explained above 4:6. There are two further blessings about which there is disagreement: <b>a)</b> Me'ein Shalosh (see above 10:2, note 1). When there is no way to resolve the doubt, one does not recite it. <b>b)</b> The mitzva of Birkat Ha-Torah is from the Torah according to most poskim, and some say it is rabbinic. In a case of doubt, according to most poskim one recites it, and this is the opinion of all the Ashkenazic poskim and some of the Sephardic poskim, among them the Ramban, the Rashba, the Mabit and Pri Ḥadash 47:1. According to the custom of many of Sephardic descent, one does not recite it; see Peninei Halakha: Prayer 10:3, note 3."},{"n":4,"en":"<b>The basis of the law</b> that we explained regarding \"safek berakhot le-hakel\" is explained briefly in the BY OḤ 67. Many wrote likewise, and so it is ruled in SA 209:3. Some say that the meaning of the rule is that there is no obligation to recite the blessing, but one who wishes to recite a blessing in a case of doubt may do so. So the Penei Yehoshua (Berakhot 12a) understood the opinion of Tosafot, and so the Nishmat Adam 5:1 understood from the opinions of the She'iltot, Tosafot and the Ḥinukh. But according to the overwhelming majority of the poskim, in a case of doubt it is forbidden to recite the blessing. According to the Rambam, who holds that the prohibition of a berakha le-vatala is from the Torah, it is clear why it is forbidden to recite a blessing in a case of doubt. But even according to most poskim, who hold that the prohibition is rabbinic, \"shev ve-al ta'aseh\" (\"sit and do nothing\") is preferable. And the Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 209:21 added that although the prohibition is rabbinic, it has a special severity, for it was based on the prohibition of \"You shall not take [God's name in vain],\" which is as severe as those offenses punishable by karet and by death at the hands of the court, and great warnings and punishments were stated regarding it (Shevuot 39a). Similarly, when there is a dispute among the poskim whether one must recite a blessing, since the blessing is a rabbinic mitzva, the halakha follows the lenient opinion, as explained in Avoda Zara 7a. So too wrote the Radbaz (1:229). According to the Kaftor Va-feraḥ (ch. 5), since there are those who hold that one must recite the blessing, one who wishes to rely on them may do so. But the overwhelming majority of the poskim hold that it is forbidden to recite the blessing. If according to <b>a clear majority</b> of the poskim one must recite the blessing and only a minority hold that one should not, some say that one must recite it, for the halakha follows the majority. This emerges from the opinions of the Radbaz (4:116), the Shakh (YD 242) and the Ḥikrei Lev, who hold that money may be exacted on the basis of the opinion of most poskim. So too wrote the Beit David (sec. 337) regarding blessings. But the accepted ruling in monetary matters is that a person may say \"kim li\" (\"I hold\") in accordance with the minority opinion, and likewise it is accepted regarding blessings that one does not recite a blessing when a minority hold that one should not. The reason is that as long as the Sages did not deliberate with one another, the majority does not override the minority. So too wrote the Maharam Ḥaviv (Get Pashut, kelal 1), Devar Moshe (vol. 1, 38), and many others. And the Maharam Ḥaviv wrote that even for the opinion of two poskim we take concern, but for one we do not. So too wrote Yabia Omer vol. 3, OḤ 16:7. So too wrote Birkat Hashem vol. 1, 2:4, who summarized the topic at length, but he wrote in note 8 that one should not protest against one who relies on the majority of poskim who hold that one should recite the blessing. The Ashkenazic poskim did not establish rules in this, and it seems that they do not take a small minority into account. When there is a <b>sefek sefeka</b> (double doubt) <b>to recite the blessing</b>, according to the Levush (OḤ sec. 17), the Leḥem Mishneh (Hilkhot Berakhot 4:6) and the Pri Ḥadash (489:8), one recites the blessing. The reason is that since we rely on a double doubt to permit a Torah prohibition, there is no need to be concerned about a blessing in vain, and only in an evenly balanced doubt is it forbidden to recite the blessing (Birkat Hashem vol. 1, 2, note 25). In contrast, according to the Leḥem Ḥamudot, the Mikhtam Le-David and the Ḥida (Maḥazik Berakha 7), one should not recite the blessing. So too wrote MB 215:20, based on the Ḥayei Adam and the Pri Megadim. The reason is that even a double doubt is considered a majority and not a certainty, for there is still a doubt that perhaps he should not recite the blessing, and why should he enter the concern of a blessing in vain? And the Mikhtam Le-David wrote that even in three doubts or more he should not recite the blessing. So too wrote Yeḥaveh Da'at 5:21, in the note. And see Birkat Hashem vol. 1, 2:9, note 25. (And see above 10, notes 6 and 12, regarding a final blessing, where one is stringent even in four doubts.) However, <b>when the custom is to recite the blessing</b>, one recites it and does not take into account the opinion of the poskim that one should not. So too wrote the Terumat Ha-deshen 34, the Rashba 3:283, and many other Rishonim and Aḥaronim. The source is in Berakhot 45a: \"Go out and see what the people are doing.\" And the Radbaz (1:229) wrote that the custom shows that this is how the halakha was decided, and there is no longer a doubt in it. Sometimes the custom preceded the dispute, and the opinion that holds that one should not recite the blessing does not nullify it. And sometimes the custom spread according to the ruling of the local rabbinic authority (mara de-atra), who, if his stature was great, at times had the strength to decide the dispute. In practice, the Rishonim were accustomed to decide doubts much more often, and with the decline of the generations, the Aḥaronim tended to be wary of deciding, and the cases in which one does not recite a blessing because of doubt multiplied. Regarding <b>blessings over mitzvot</b> the law is different: if the halakha was decided that one must perform the mitzva, the blessing follows that decision, as explained above 2:3, that even when one washes the hands only up to the ends of the fingers, one recites the blessing. So too wrote the Radbaz 1:229. And see the Har Bracha expanded commentary (Harḥavot) here. <b>According to the custom of those of Sephardic descent</b>: when the SA decided to recite the blessing, and there are those who hold that one should not recite it — according to the Beit David, in all types of blessings one recites the blessing; according to the Ḥida and the Ben Ish Ḥai, one does not recite it. So too wrote Yabia Omer vol. 5, OḤ 42:3. And when there is a dispute and the holy Arizal decided to recite the blessing, according to the Ḥida one recites it, and this is the opinion of many (see Birkat Hashem vol. 1, 2:14), whereas Yabia Omer (vol. 2, 25:12) holds that one should not recite it."}]},{"slug":"10-12-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-04/","title_he":"ד – האם מותר ליהנות כאשר ספק אם בירך","title_en":"Is It Permitted to Derive Benefit When in Doubt Whether One Recited a Blessing?","body_en":"<p>If one wished to eat something and was in doubt whether he had already recited a blessing over it, since in doubtful cases of blessings we are lenient, he should eat without a blessing. If it is possible to find a solution, it is proper to do so in order to resolve the doubt. For example, if there is a person there who needs to recite the same blessing, he should ask him to have in mind to fulfill his obligation with his blessing, answer Amen after him, and eat immediately. And regarding foods whose final blessing is Borei Nefashot, if he goes out of his house for a short walk, an interruption will be created, and thus when he returns he will be able to recite a first blessing again over the food he wishes to eat (above 9:7). He is not considered one who caused himself to recite an unnecessary blessing, since he did so in order to resolve the doubt (above, section 2). In any event, when there is no solution, according to the letter of the law it is permitted to eat, for only when there is certainty that one must recite a blessing is it obligatory to recite it, and when there is no certainty, it is not possible to recite it. One who wishes to be stringent with himself should refrain from eating.</p><p>Some say that when a person is in doubt whether he must recite a blessing, he may say the blessing, and instead of saying the sanctified Name he should say its Aramaic translation, for example, he should say \"Barukh Raḥmana Melekh ha-olam\" (\"Blessed is the Merciful One, King of the universe\") and continue saying the blessing in Hebrew. In this way, on the one hand he will not sin by uttering the name of Heaven in vain, and on the other hand he will fulfill the obligation of the blessing, since even one who recites a blessing in another language fulfills his obligation (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 202:3). Many disagreed with this, holding that the prohibition of a berakha le-vatala does not depend on God's name, but on the very fact that a person intends to utter a blessing when he is not obligated in it. And just as one who swears a vain oath mentioning God's name in a foreign language transgresses a Torah prohibition, so too one who recites a blessing mentioning God's name in a foreign language transgresses the prohibition of a berakha le-vatala (Responsa Rabbi Akiva Eiger, Kamma 25). Since the matter is disputed, it is preferable to refrain from it (Yeḥaveh Da'at 6:15).</p><p>Some Aḥaronim recommended that whenever, because of a doubt, it is impossible to recite a blessing, it is good to think the blessing in one's heart. God sees into the heart that he wishes to give thanks before Him, and at the same time there is no concern of a blessing in vain in this (Pri Megadim, Rabbi Akiva Eiger, Ben Ish Ḥai, Matot 14. According to the Rambam one fulfills the obligation through thought, and even according to his view there is no concern of a blessing in vain in this).<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"The essential law, that he may eat, is explained in the Rambam and copied by the SA (167:9; 209:3), and this is the opinion of other Rishonim and Aḥaronim; so too wrote the Rema 210:2. Some hold that it is preferable that he not eat at all, so that he will not derive benefit in a situation where perhaps he should have recited a blessing (Devar Moshe vol. 3, OḤ p. 14, col. 4). The Ben Ish Ḥai wrote that when possible it is preferable that he not eat (Naso 11), and after the fact he should think the blessing in his heart (Balak 7). But as stated, in practice he may derive benefit (see Responsa Ḥazon Ovadia vol. 2, p. 398). However, if it is possible to find a way to resolve the doubt, this is a measure of piety (middat ḥasidut), as written in Arukh Ha-shulḥan 202:2. And see Piskei Teshuvot 209:7. For example, if he has the possibility of bringing a food that he had not thought at all of eating, whose blessing is the same, he can recite the blessing over it and exempt himself from the doubt. And when the doubt is whether he ate a kezayit, if he wishes to be meticulous, he should eat another kezayit and can then recite the final blessing with certainty. And when there is no solution, those who wish to be meticulous may think the blessing in their heart."}]},{"slug":"10-12-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-05/","title_he":"ה – מאה ברכות","title_en":"One Hundred Blessings","body_en":"<p>A person is obligated to recite one hundred blessings every day (Menaḥot 43b; SA 46:3). King David instituted this at a time when there was a plague, and one hundred souls of Israel died each day. David understood through the holy spirit (ru'aḥ ha-kodesh) that the plague was caused because people were not blessing God in completeness, with one hundred blessings every day, and he arose and instituted for Israel that they should recite one hundred blessings every day (Bamidbar Rabba 18:21; Tur OḤ 46).</p><p>Men are obligated in this mitzva, for they are obligated to pray three prayers a day, and through this, together with the meals, they reach more than one hundred blessings every day. But women are exempt from three prayers every day, and consequently they do not have the possibility of reaching one hundred blessings.</p><p>On Shabbatot and Yamim Tovim, when there are fewer blessings in the Amida prayer, one must add several more blessings over various kinds of spices and foods (Menaḥot 43b). When this is not possible, such as on Yom Kippur and other fast days, when all the blessings of enjoyment over food are lacking, one may have in mind to include the blessings one hears from those called up to the Torah and for the Haftara. And when necessary, one may also include the blessings one hears from the ḥazan in the repetition of the Amida (MB 46:14).<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"The count of the blessings is as follows: three Amida prayers – 57. The blessings of Keriat Shema evening and morning, and the blessings of Pesukei De-zimra – 9. Ha-mapil, Birkhot Ha-shaḥar, the blessing of Al Netilat Yadayim, Asher Yatzar, Elohai Neshama, and Birkhot Ha-Torah (according to those who hold that they are two), tzitzit and tefillin (according to those who hold that one recites one blessing) – 23. Total so far: 89. (And for those of Ashkenazic descent, who recite two blessings over tefillin, and according to those who hold that Birkhot Ha-Torah are three – 91.) And if one eats three meals with bread in the course of a day, each meal having six blessings, we reach 107, and this is without counting the times one relieves oneself and recites Asher Yatzar, and the blessings one recites at the beginning and the end over what one tastes and drinks during the day. On Shabbat, 36 blessings are lacking in the 3 Amida prayers (7 instead of 19), and the blessing for tefillin; on the other hand, in Musaf there are another 7 blessings, and another 3 in the Kiddushim; in total 27 blessings are lacking. Thus on Shabbat there are at least 80. And in order to complete one hundred blessings, one must add more blessings over fragrance and over what one tastes and drinks outside the framework of the meal (see above 3:6), and Asher Yatzar after using the toilet. And if he does not manage to reach one hundred blessings, he should have in mind, when he answers Amen to the blessings of those called up to the Torah, that they be included for him. (The day is counted as a full day-and-night cycle, that is, the night and afterwards the day, and so the BY, the MA and the MB calculated. And so it is evident, for if it were not so, there would be no difficulty reaching one hundred blessings on Shabbat and Yom Kippur.)"}]},{"slug":"10-12-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-06/","title_he":"ו – כללים בנוסח הברכות","title_en":"Rules Regarding the Wording of Blessings","body_en":"<p>There are blessings that the Sages instituted in a short form, containing a single expression of thanks without lengthy wording, such as Birkhot Ha-shaḥar and the blessings of enjoyment (birkhot ha-nehenin). And there are blessings that were instituted in a long form, which include many details. In a short blessing one says \"Barukh Ata\" (\"Blessed are You\") etc. once, whereas a long blessing opens with \"Barukh Ata,\" and at the conclusion of the blessing one again says \"Barukh Ata,\" as for example in the blessings of Asher Yatzar and Me'ein Shalosh. And when it is a long blessing that is always adjacent to another blessing, it does not open with \"Barukh,\" for the preceding blessing that opens with \"Barukh\" is considered its opening (Pesaḥim 104b). Therefore, in the blessings of the Amida prayer and of Birkat Ha-mazon, only the first blessing opens with \"Barukh,\" and in all the rest, although they are long, one says \"Barukh Ata\" only at their conclusion (see above 4:2). And there are a few blessings of thanksgiving which, although they are sometimes said not adjacent to another blessing, do not open with \"Barukh,\" such as the blessing Elohai Neshama that one says in the morning (SA 6:3; and see there MB 12).</p><p>One should not change the wording of the blessings instituted by the Sages, and if one changed it, as long as he said the four fundamental elements of every blessing, he has fulfilled his obligation. These are the four fundamental elements: a) \"Barukh\" (\"Blessed\"); b) God's name; c) that He is King of the universe; <br />d) the specific content of the blessing; and there are blessings whose content includes several essential details, as in the blessings of Birkat Ha-mazon (above 4:5). If one omitted one of these elements, he has not fulfilled his obligation (Berakhot 40b; SA 214:1; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 185:2; MB 68:1).</p><p>When there is a series of blessings recited in sequence, such as Birkat Ha-mazon, or the blessings of Keriat Shema, or the Shemoneh Esrei prayer, there is no need to mention His kingship in all the blessings, for since one already said this in the first blessing, all the blessings recited afterwards in sequence rely on it. But God's name must be mentioned in all the blessings, because the foundation of a blessing is turning to God, and therefore one must mention His name.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-12-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-07/","title_he":"ז – שניים שאוכלים יחד – האם טוב שאחד יברך?","title_en":"Two People Eating Together – Is It Good That One Recite the Blessing?","body_en":"<p>When two people come to eat bread together, since they have established themselves to eat together, it is good that one recite Ha-motzi and exempt the other. This is because a blessing said on behalf of two people is more important, and it contains a greater expression of thanksgiving to God (Berakhot 42b; see above 5:1). And this is the practice at Shabbat meals: since they have gathered to eat together, the head of the household recites Ha-motzi on behalf of everyone, for \"in the multitude of people is the King's glory\" (Berakhot 53a; SA 167:11; above 3:4. And see above 1:10 regarding the laws of one who fulfills the obligation on behalf of another).</p><p>But if each one is eating for himself, and they are not waiting for one another, it is better that each recite the blessing for himself. And if one does not know how to recite the blessing, even though each of them is eating for himself, the one who knows how to recite the blessing should recite it aloud in order to exempt his companion who does not know how to recite it (see SA 167:13).</p><p>Regarding eating fruit and other foods, if they did not establish themselves to eat together, according to all opinions it is proper that each recite the blessing for himself. And if they established themselves to eat together, some say that it is preferable that one recite the blessing aloud and exempt the other (SA 213:1). Others say that only eating bread or drinking wine is considered something that unites the group, and with them it is good that one recite the blessing on behalf of everyone. But other foods and beverages are not so important that people unite to eat or drink them, and therefore even when they sit to eat them together, as long as they are not within the framework of a meal with bread, it is preferable that each recite the blessing for himself (Rema there; MB 11). In practice, since each of the customs has its own advantage, those eating may choose whether to prefer that one recite the blessing for everyone or that each recite the blessing for himself.</p><p>Regarding the final blessing, it is agreed that it is preferable that each recite the blessing for himself; but if there is someone there who is concerned that perhaps he does not remember the blessing properly, or who is in doubt whether he ate the amount that obligates a blessing, it is proper that one recite the blessing aloud in order to exempt him (SA 213:1. And see above 5:6 regarding zimun).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-12-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-08/","title_he":"ח – חובת עניית אמן","title_en":"The Obligation to Answer Amen","body_en":"<p>One who hears a Jew recite a blessing is obligated to answer \"Amen\" after him (SA 215:2). And even if he did not hear the entire blessing but only its end, he is obligated to answer Amen (Ḥayei Adam; MB 215:6). And if he did not hear the blessing, but he knows which blessing is being recited, and understood from the movements of the lips of the one reciting it that he finished the blessing, he may answer Amen after him but is not obligated to (Be'ur Halakha there; Rema 124:8).</p><p>It is forbidden to answer Amen after one who recites a blessing in vain (SA 215:4). But one who heard a blessing from someone who recites it according to his custom, even though the listener is not accustomed to say this blessing, is obligated to answer Amen to it, since the one who recited it acted properly according to his custom. For example, one who is not accustomed to recite a blessing over Hallel on Rosh Ḥodesh must answer Amen after one who is accustomed to recite it.<sup>7</sup></p><p>One who hears a blessing from a minor who has reached the age of training (ḥinukh) for blessings, that is, he understands the meaning of the blessing and knows how to say it properly, is obligated to answer Amen after him (SA 215:3). Children generally reach this at the age of five or six. And if a minor who has not yet reached this stage recites a blessing, one does not answer Amen after him, but one praises him for having recited the blessing nicely, in order to accustom him to recite blessings.</p><p>When teaching a child to recite a blessing, one accustoms him to say the wording of the blessing with God's name, and there is no prohibition of a berakha le-vatala in this, since he does so for the sake of learning. But one does not answer Amen to a blessing intended for practice (SA 215:3).</p><p>One who hears a non-Jew recite a blessing in a place where it is fitting to say it, such as before or after eating, should answer Amen after him, since his intention is for the sake of Heaven. But if there is a concern that he is directing it toward idolatry, even though he says the blessing in our wording, one should not answer Amen after him (SA and Rema 215:2; MB 12). Above, in chapter 1, halakha 9, the mitzva of answering Amen and its great virtue are explained.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"See Peninei Halakha: Zemanim 1:12, that the custom of those of Sephardic descent who lived in the Land of Israel and its surroundings is not to recite a blessing over Hallel on Rosh Ḥodesh, in accordance with the opinion of Rashi and the Rambam. And the custom of those of Ashkenazic descent and most of those of North African descent is to recite the blessing, in accordance with the opinion of most Rishonim. Yabia Omer vol. 1, 29:9 wrote that a Sephardic Jew should not answer Amen to this blessing. But I heard from Rabbi Eliyahu that this blessing should not be considered in vain, since this is what the one reciting it must do according to his custom, and consequently the listener must answer Amen. So too wrote Halikhot Shlomo 4:19 and Birkat Hashem vol. 1, 6:12. And so it is explained in Birkei Yosef 196:4 and Har Tzvi 1:38."}]},{"slug":"10-12-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-09/","title_he":"ט – האם יוצאים בשמיעה ברמקול","title_en":"Does One Fulfill One's Obligation by Hearing Through a Loudspeaker?","body_en":"<p>The poskim disagree whether one who hears a blessing through a telephone or a loudspeaker fulfills his obligation. Some say that since the sound came forth from the power of the one reciting the blessing, and the listener heard it immediately, even though the sound passed through an electrical device, its law is like the voice of the one reciting the blessing, by hearing which one can fulfill one's obligation. Others say that since the sound heard is not the original voice of the one reciting the blessing, but rather the electrical device picked it up as electrical signals and afterwards converted it again into a new sound, it is like hearing the sound of a machine, through which one does not fulfill one's obligation.</p><p>In practice, one should take into account the opinion of the stringent poskim, and not perform mitzvot that depend on hearing by means of electrical devices. However, when there is no choice, it is preferable to perform the mitzva by means of electrical devices than to lose it entirely. For example, one who is in a place where he cannot hear the reading of the Megilla or Havdala should preferably hear it over the telephone or in a live radio broadcast. And in zimun, when there are many people and it is difficult to hear the one leading the zimun without a loudspeaker, it is good to make use of it (in zimun there is more room for leniency, as explained above 5:6, note 10).</p><p>The poskim disagree regarding the law of a hard-of-hearing person who uses a hearing aid. Some say that he does not fulfill his obligation, since he does not actually hear with his ear. On the other hand, some say that hearing through the device is like hearing through the ear, since in practice he heard the sound. And some say that for the mitzva of hearing the shofar one must hear the shofar directly without any intermediary, but other mitzvot can be fulfilled by hearing through a device. In practice, for rabbinic mitzvot one may rely on the lenient opinion, and for Torah mitzvot, when possible, it is preferable to hear without the device.</p><p>One who hears blessings and Kaddish through a loudspeaker must answer Amen. And one who hears through a telephone or a live radio broadcast should preferably answer Amen, but is not obligated to.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"Some say that one who hears through an electrical device is like one who hears a recording: since he did not hear the original sound, he cannot fulfill mitzvot that depend on hearing. So wrote Mishpetei Uziel 1:21; 3:34; Kol Mevaser 2:25; Rabbi Menaḥem Mendel Schneerson, Sha'arei Halakha U-minhag OḤ 291; Rabbi Shlomo Zalman Auerbach in Minḥat Shlomo 1:9; Mishneh Halakhot 4:85. On the other hand, some say that since in practice one who hears through a device hears the sound, he fulfills the obligation of mitzvot that depend on hearing. So wrote Responsa Yam Ha-gadol 29; the Ḥazon Ish as cited there in Minḥat Shlomo; and so wrote my friend Rabbi Re'em Ha-Kohen in Badei Ha-aron OḤ 21, and that this is the opinion of Rabbi Lichtenstein. In principle, so wrote Igrot Moshe OḤ 2:108, but in practice in OḤ 4:91 he was lenient only regarding rabbinic mitzvot. One of the foundations of the lenient opinion's reasoning is that even hearing by means of the ear does not occur directly, for the sound passes through sound waves in the air, and afterwards is received by slight vibrations within the ear, and those vibrations are received as electrical signals in the cerebral cortex, which are afterwards decoded in the brain into the hearing we are familiar with. And seemingly there is no difference if the sound also passes along the way through an electrical device. The opinion of our master Rav Kook in Oraḥ Mishpat 48 is that in principle one can fulfill one's obligation by hearing through electrical devices, and only with hearing the shofar does one not fulfill it, since it must be direct and not an echoing sound (kol havara). So too wrote Rabbi Frank in Mikra'ei Kodesh, Purim 11, regarding the reading of the Megilla; Tzitz Eliezer 8:11; Beit Avi 3:92. Regarding a cochlear implant, some say that even the stringent poskim would agree to be lenient, since hearing through it is similar to hearing through the ear (Dr. Berama [?], Teḥumin 24; Rabbi Rabinovitch, Teḥumin 31). And it seems that when it becomes possible to hear through the devices or the implant exactly as in normal hearing, the stringent poskim will agree to be lenient even regarding hearing the shofar. Regarding answering Amen to Kaddish and blessings, if it is through radio or telephone, since according to most poskim one does not fulfill one's obligation through this, it seems that there is no obligation to answer, but it is a mitzva to answer. And if the sound is heard by means of a loudspeaker, it seems that there is an obligation to answer, because according to many one can fulfill one's obligation through this, and consequently it is like hearing an ordinary blessing. And even according to those who disagree with them, there is at least a mitzva to answer."}]},{"slug":"10-12-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-10/","title_he":"י – האוכל איסור","title_en":"One Who Eats Forbidden Food","body_en":"<p>One does not recite a blessing over eating a food that is forbidden to be eaten, neither a first blessing nor a final blessing, for how can one thank God for an enjoyment that came into his hands through a transgression? And if he recites a blessing, his blessing will not be praise but rather blasphemy and an affront to the honor of Heaven. There is no difference whether the food is forbidden by Torah law, such as meat that was not slaughtered according to halakha, or forbidden by rabbinic law, such as fruit from which terumot and ma'asrot were not separated at a time when most of Israel is outside the Land; in any case one does not recite a blessing over them (Mishna Berakhot 45a; SA 196:1).</p><p>Likewise, one who steals food, in addition to having to return the stolen item, is forbidden to recite a blessing over it, and if he recites a blessing it is not a blessing but blasphemy, as it is stated (Tehillim 10:3): \"The robber who blesses has blasphemed the Lord\" (based on Sanhedrin 6b).</p><p>One who eats kosher food at a time when he is forbidden to eat, for example one who eats on a fast day, or on Motza'ei Shabbat before Havdala, or before the Shaḥarit prayer, or on Sukkot outside the sukka, since the food itself is kosher and only the time or place of eating is improper, must recite a blessing over his enjoyment of the kosher food.</p><p>One who is in danger and, in order to save himself, must eat forbidden foods, such as nevelot and tereifot (meat from animals that were not properly slaughtered or that had fatal defects), must recite a blessing over them. And although he would prefer not to need to eat them, since in practice he eats them in a permitted manner, for saving a life overrides all the mitzvot, he must recite a blessing over his enjoyment. However, if he finds the forbidden food repugnant, for example he is forced to eat pork and feels disgust at it, he should not recite a blessing, since in practice he does not enjoy it (SA 196:2; 204:9; MB 204:48).<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"<b>One who eats something forbidden</b>: according to the Ra'avad and the Rosh, since he derived enjoyment, he must recite a blessing. According to the Rambam, the Ramban and most Rishonim, he is forbidden to recite a blessing, for if he recites a blessing he blasphemes. The BY 196:1 ruled in accordance with their words, because this is how it appears from the Talmudic discussion. But regarding a sick person who is forced to eat something forbidden, the Rashbatz and the BY wrote that since he eats it in a permitted manner, even the Rambam would agree in this case with the Ra'avad that he must recite a blessing. And this is the opinion of most poskim, as cited in Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 204:41. According to the Ramah (Rabbi Meir Ha-Levi Abulafia), in any case one should not recite a blessing over a forbidden food, and so too wrote the Baḥ and the Derisha; but since the opinion of most poskim, Sephardic and Ashkenazic, is to recite the blessing, and it is also a double doubt (sefek sefeka), and this is also the opinion of the SA, that is what I wrote above in practice. Over <b>kosher food</b> eaten at a time or in a place where it is forbidden to eat, one should recite a blessing, as cited in Piskei Teshuvot 196:3. The poskim disagree regarding one who ate <b>a forbidden food unintentionally</b>: according to the Taz and the Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav, he must recite a final blessing, and the opinion of MB 196:4 inclines toward this. Many disagree (Rambam 1:19, and so it is implied by the SA, and so too wrote the Ma'amar Mordekhai, the Arukh Ha-shulḥan and Kaf Ha-ḥayim 3). And in doubtful cases of blessings we are lenient."}]},{"slug":"10-12-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-11/","title_he":"יא – המברך באיסור האם יאכל","title_en":"One Who Recited a Blessing Over Something Forbidden – Should He Eat?","body_en":"<p>If one erred and recited a blessing over a food that is forbidden to be eaten, such as over tereifa meat, or ḥametz on Pesaḥ, he should not eat any of it, and his blessing was in vain. And even if the prohibition of eating is only rabbinic, he should not taste it at all.</p><p>Likewise, if one ate a meat food and within an hour erred and recited a blessing over a dairy food, he should not taste the dairy food, since according to all opinions this is forbidden by rabbinic law. But if, before reciting the blessing over the dairy food, he had managed to eat a pareve food and drink, whereby his mouth was cleansed of remnants of the meat, or an hour had passed since he finished eating the meat, since there are Rishonim who permit eating dairy at that point, he should taste a little of the dairy food, so that his blessing will not be in vain (see Peninei Halakha: Kashrut 26:2, notes 3 and 5).</p><p>If one erred on Motza'ei Shabbat before Havdala and recited a blessing over a food, and before he began to eat remembered that it is forbidden to eat before Havdala, he should taste a little of the food. Since there is no prohibition in the food itself, in order that his blessing not turn out to be in vain, it is preferable that he taste a little after his blessing. The same law applies to one who erred and recited a blessing over a food before Kiddush (Rema 271:5; MB 26).</p><p>Likewise, if one erred and recited a blessing over something that is not eaten because of a custom, he should taste it, so that his blessing will not be in vain. Therefore, one who mistakenly recites a blessing over meat during the Nine Days should taste a little of the meat. This is because the prohibition of eating meat during the Nine Days is due to custom. Furthermore, there is no joy in a mere taste, and therefore it barely detracts from the mourning.</p><p>Likewise, one who erred and recited a blessing over a food before the Shaḥarit prayer should taste a little, because there is no expression of arrogance in this tasting, for he tastes only in order to be saved from a blessing in vain.</p><p>If one forgot that it is a fast day and recited Shehakol over water, according to most poskim he should not drink. This is because the prohibition of eating and drinking on a fast day is clear, and one should not take an action and transgress this prohibition.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"However, according to Birkat Ha-bayit 19:15 and Rabbi Ovadia Yosef, he should drink a little, so that his blessing will not be in vain. Their reason is that the SA 215:4 ruled for halakha that the prohibition of a berakha le-vatala is from the Torah, whereas the prohibition of eating on a fast day is rabbinic. However, the overwhelming majority of the Rishonim hold that a berakha le-vatala is forbidden by rabbinic law. Furthermore, the fasts are from divrei kabbala (the Prophets and Writings), and some say that divrei kabbala are like Torah law. Furthermore, perhaps the fasts have a Torah aspect in that they are like a vow. Furthermore, it is preferable to transgress through passive inaction (shev ve-al ta'aseh) than through an active deed (kum aseh). So ruled Da'at Torah 568:1, Sha'arei Teshuva 1, and Birkat Hashem vol. 2, 1, note 95. So too I wrote in Peninei Halakha: Zemanim 7:6, note 7. If one recited a blessing over fruit and remembered that he had not separated terumot and ma'asrot from them, he should separate them and say the entire wording of the separation, and afterwards eat, for since the entire separation is for the sake of eating, it is not an interruption (and see Piskei Teshuvot 167:10, notes 79-80, and Sha'arei Ha-berakha 13:19-20)."}]},{"slug":"10-12-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-12/","title_he":"יב – נתינת מאכל לפני מי שלא יברך","title_en":"Giving Food to One Who Will Not Recite a Blessing","body_en":"<p>When a person offers his friend food, he bears a responsibility to see to it that he recites a blessing, for otherwise it turns out that he assists him in stumbling into the prohibition of eating something without a blessing (SA 169:2).</p><p>But if his friend is secular, and there is a reasonable concern that if he asks him to recite a blessing he will be offended, he should offer him food and drink without asking him to recite a blessing. For if he offends him, he will cause him to stumble into a more severe prohibition, for he will cause tension and hatred between them, and he may even cause him to distance himself from Torah and mitzvot. However, one should not be overly concerned that the guest will be offended, since in practice most Jews who are not defined as religious are happy to recite a blessing, or at least to answer Amen, when they are guests of their religious friend or relative. Therefore, it is generally possible to gently suggest to the guest that he recite a blessing, and at the very least the host can recite the blessing aloud, and by the guest answering Amen, he will be considered to some extent a partner in the blessing (see Igrot Moshe OḤ 5:13; Minḥat Shlomo 1:35; Shevet Ha-levi 4:17; Piskei Teshuvot 169:3-4).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-12-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-12-13/","title_he":"יג – דין הטועה בברכתו","title_en":"The Law Regarding One Who Errs in His Blessing","body_en":"<p>If one took a cup of water in his hand, and while saying \"Barukh Ata Hashem Eloheinu Melekh ha-olam\" (\"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe\") he erred and thought he was holding wine, and intended to recite Ha-gafen, and then remembered that it was water and concluded \"she-ha-kol nihya bi-dvaro\" (\"by whose word all things came to be\"), he has fulfilled his obligation (Rashi and Tosafot).</p><p>And even if he remembered his error only after he finished the blessing and said \"borei pri ha-gafen\" (\"who creates the fruit of the vine\"), and immediately remembered and said, within the time it takes to speak a few words (tokh kedei dibbur), \"she-ha-kol nihya bi-dvaro,\" since in practice he corrected himself, he has fulfilled his obligation. There are some Rishonim who hold that since he erred in the blessing itself, both in his intention and in his speech, he has not fulfilled his obligation (as implied by Rashi and Tosafot). However, because of the doubt, most Rishonim decided that he has fulfilled his obligation (the Ra'avad and the Rosh, based on the Geonim and the Rif; and so too wrote SA 209:2; MB 5-6).</p><p>And if he said the entire blessing over the water properly, and after he finished he became confused and thought that he needed to correct the blessing, and again concluded \"borei pri ha-gafen\": some say that he has not fulfilled his obligation, since he decided to conclude the blessing over wine, and the blessing of Ha-gafen does not exempt water (the Ra'avad and the Razah). Others say that since at first he said the blessing properly, he already fulfilled his obligation, and the fact that afterwards he \"corrected\" himself cannot invalidate the blessing that had already been concluded properly (the Ramban, based on the Geonim). And since in doubtful cases of blessings we are lenient, he has fulfilled his obligation.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"If one took a cup of water in his hand, and began and said \"Barukh Ata Hashem Eloheinu Melekh ha-olam\" with the thought of concluding with Shehakol, and erred and concluded \"borei pri ha-gafen\": according to most poskim he has not fulfilled his obligation, since in practice he said a mistaken blessing (the Ra'avad, the Rosh and the Rashba). According to the Rambam, his initial thought at the beginning of the blessing to say it properly is effective for him to fulfill his obligation, even though in practice he did not say the proper wording. And some, because of the doubt, ruled that he should not recite the blessing again (SA 209:1). However, in practice, many ruled that he has not fulfilled his obligation, and he must recite the blessing again (Taz, MA, MB 209:1)."}]}]});
